    "id": 10,
    "category": "Hematology: Mixed IDA + ACD",
    "questionText": "A 72-year-old man with chronic heart failure, rheumatoid arthritis, and chronic GI blood loss from NSAID-induced gastritis presents with worsening fatigue. Vitals: BP 124/76, HR 92. Exam: pale conjunctiva, RA changes in hands, no acute distress. Labs: Hgb 8.4, MCV 75, RDW 19.5, ferritin 140 (within normal range), TIBC 250, transferrin sat 12%, serum iron 30, CRP 28 (elevated), soluble transferrin receptor (sTfR) markedly elevated. Which is the most likely diagnosis?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Pure anemia of chronic disease only — no need for iron supplementation", "explanation": "Incorrect. In a pure ACD picture, the elevated sTfR would NOT be present — sTfR is a marker of iron-deficient erythropoiesis that is NOT affected by inflammation (unlike ferritin). The markedly elevated sTfR here indicates that despite the normal-range ferritin (falsely elevated by inflammation), the marrow is iron-starved. This would be the correct answer if sTfR were normal and ferritin were appropriately elevated without need for iron." },
      { "text": "Pure iron deficiency anemia only — ignore the inflammatory markers", "explanation": "Incorrect. The elevated CRP and chronic inflammatory conditions clearly indicate concurrent inflammation/ACD. Ferritin in pure IDA would be markedly LOW (<30); a 'normal range' ferritin in this setting is artificially elevated by the inflammation, masking the iron deficiency. The combination is MIXED IDA + ACD. This would be the correct answer if there were no inflammatory disease and ferritin were clearly low." },
      { "text": "Mixed iron deficiency anemia AND anemia of chronic disease — IV iron supplementation indicated alongside disease control", "explanation": "Correct. This is a classic MIXED IDA + ACD presentation. Clues: (1) elevated SOLUBLE TRANSFERRIN RECEPTOR (sTfR) — unaffected by inflammation, so it stays elevated in iron deficiency even when ferritin is masked; (2) ferritin is 'normal' but disproportionately low for the degree of inflammation (cutoff for IDA in setting of inflammation is often raised to ferritin <100); (3) low transferrin saturation and low serum iron; (4) chronic GI losses providing a mechanism for iron deficiency. Management: IV IRON (oral absorption is impaired by hepcidin in ACD) PLUS optimization of underlying inflammatory disease. Soluble transferrin receptor (or sTfR/log ferritin ratio) is the workhorse test for detecting concurrent iron deficiency in patients with ACD." },
      { "text": "Beta-thalassemia minor with concurrent inflammation", "explanation": "Incorrect. Beta-thal minor has normal iron studies (normal ferritin, normal TIBC, normal transferrin sat) and a normal RDW. This patient has an elevated RDW and the iron studies are abnormal with elevated sTfR — beta-thal does not fit. This would be the correct answer in a Mediterranean patient with normal iron studies, normal RDW, and target cells." },
      { "text": "Sideroblastic anemia from lead exposure", "explanation": "Incorrect. Sideroblastic anemia shows HIGH ferritin and HIGH transferrin saturation, with ringed sideroblasts on bone marrow. Lead poisoning shows basophilic stippling and is suggested by lead exposure history. Neither fits this patient. This would be the correct answer with elevated lead level and basophilic stippling." }
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 2,
    "clinicalPearl": "Key takeaway: When IDA and ACD coexist, ferritin is unreliable (inflammation falsely elevates it). SOLUBLE TRANSFERRIN RECEPTOR (sTfR) is the key test — unaffected by inflammation. Use IV iron (hepcidin blocks oral absorption in ACD).",
