    "id": 12,
    "category": "Hematology: Reticulocyte Response after Iron Repletion",
    "questionText": "A 35-year-old woman with heavy menstrual bleeding and confirmed IDA was started on oral ferrous sulfate 325 mg TID 8 days ago. She returns for follow-up. Vitals: BP 110/68, HR 78. Exam: improved energy. Labs: Hgb 10.5 (baseline 8.5), MCV 76 (baseline 72), reticulocyte count 8% (baseline 1.5%). She tolerates oral iron well. Which best describes the next expected lab finding 4-6 weeks from now if she continues therapy?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Reticulocytosis will persist indefinitely throughout therapy at 8%", "explanation": "Incorrect. Reticulocytosis peaks 7-10 days after iron repletion begins and then DECLINES as the anemia corrects and EPO drive falls. Persistent high reticulocyte count at 6 weeks would suggest ongoing iron loss exceeding repletion. This would be the correct answer if the patient had ongoing blood loss not yet controlled." },
      { "text": "Hemoglobin and MCV will both rise, with ferritin gradually normalizing as the iron deficit closes", "explanation": "Correct. The expected response to iron therapy is well-defined: (1) RETICULOCYTOSIS within 5-10 days (already seen — 8% at day 8), peaking ~day 10; (2) HEMOGLOBIN rises ~1 g/dL per week for 6-8 weeks until normalized; (3) MCV normalizes more slowly as new normocytic RBCs replace microcytic ones over ~8-12 weeks; (4) FERRITIN/iron stores require 3-6 months of continued therapy after Hgb normalizes to fully repolish stores. The general rule: continue iron therapy for at least 3-6 months AFTER Hgb normalizes to replete iron stores. Failure to mount this response after 4 weeks of compliance with adequate-dose oral iron warrants reevaluation (compliance? absorption? ongoing loss? wrong diagnosis?)." },
      { "text": "Hgb will plateau at 10.5 and not rise further without IV iron", "explanation": "Incorrect. Oral iron at the dose given is generally sufficient for repletion in uncomplicated IDA — the patient is already responding well (Hgb up 2 g/dL in 8 days, brisk reticulocytosis). IV iron is reserved for malabsorption, intolerance, or rapid repletion needs. This would be the correct answer in a patient with celiac disease or persistent inability to absorb oral iron." },
      { "text": "Hgb will rise but reticulocyte count will paradoxically fall to ZERO due to suppression", "explanation": "Incorrect. Reticulocyte count will fall as the anemia corrects (lowering EPO drive) but will not drop to zero — it returns to normal baseline (~1-2%). This would be the correct answer in pure red cell aplasia (parvovirus B19 in sickle cell), where reticulocytes are completely absent." },
      { "text": "Pernicious anemia features will emerge requiring B12 replacement", "explanation": "Incorrect. There is no indication that this patient has B12 deficiency; her response to iron is appropriate for pure IDA. Adding B12 without indication is unnecessary. This would be the correct answer if the patient had concurrent B12 deficiency revealed by macrocytosis emerging during treatment." }
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "clinicalPearl": "Key takeaway: IDA response to iron — reticulocytosis at 5-10 days, Hgb ~1 g/dL/week, MCV normalizes slowly over 8-12 weeks. Continue iron 3-6 months AFTER Hgb normalization to replete stores.",
    "pdfPage": 12,
    "pdfQuote": "reticulocyte"
