    "id": 14,
    "category": "Hematology: Anemia of CKD Non-Responder",
    "questionText": "A 70-year-old dialysis-dependent patient on darbepoetin weekly for the past 4 months has not achieved a Hgb response (current Hgb 9.0, target 10-11.5). The dose of darbepoetin has been escalated twice without effect. Vitals: BP 144/82, HR 78. Exam: pale conjunctiva, dialysis fistula intact. Labs: ferritin 65, transferrin sat 14%, CRP 22 (elevated), B12 and folate normal, no overt blood loss. Which is the most likely cause of ESA hyporesponsiveness?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Aluminum toxicity from old dialysate", "explanation": "Incorrect. Aluminum toxicity is rare with modern dialysis water purification and would more characteristically cause microcytic anemia with neurological symptoms (dialysis encephalopathy). The iron studies here clearly suggest iron deficiency. This would be the correct answer in a dialysis patient with neurologic symptoms and exposure to non-purified dialysate." },
      { "text": "Absolute iron deficiency (low ferritin <100, low transferrin sat <20%) AND functional iron deficiency from inflammation — supplement with IV iron", "explanation": "Correct. The most common causes of ESA hyporesponsiveness in CKD are: (1) ABSOLUTE iron deficiency (ferritin <100, transferrin sat <20%) — here the patient has both, and (2) FUNCTIONAL iron deficiency from inflammation (ferritin can appear adequate but iron is sequestered by hepcidin, marker = transferrin sat <20% with normal/high ferritin). Both forms are present in this patient (ferritin 65 + low TSAT + elevated CRP). Management: IV iron (oral iron poorly absorbed in CKD due to hepcidin); continue/optimize ESA; address underlying inflammation if possible. Other ESA hyporesponsiveness causes to consider: occult blood loss, hyperparathyroidism, malignancy, infection, B12/folate deficiency (already excluded here), aluminum toxicity (rare), pure red cell aplasia from anti-EPO antibodies (rare). Goal: replete iron, then reassess Hgb response." },
      { "text": "Hyperthyroidism causing rapid metabolism of darbepoetin", "explanation": "Incorrect. Thyroid dysfunction is not a typical cause of ESA hyporesponsiveness. This would be the correct answer if the patient had clear hyperthyroid features (weight loss, palpitations, heat intolerance, suppressed TSH)." },
      { "text": "Anti-EPO antibody-mediated pure red cell aplasia (rare ESA complication)", "explanation": "Incorrect. While this is a recognized rare complication of subcutaneous ESA (especially with certain epoetin formulations historically), it would present with profound reticulocytopenia (essentially zero) and rapidly worsening anemia despite escalating ESA doses — the picture would be more dramatic. Iron-related causes are far more common and should be excluded first. This would be the correct answer with retic count near zero and confirmed anti-EPO antibodies." },
      { "text": "Switch to oral ferrous sulfate to improve iron stores", "explanation": "Incorrect. Oral iron is poorly absorbed in CKD because chronic inflammation upregulates hepcidin, which blocks ferroportin and prevents enterocyte iron release into circulation. Standard of care in CKD with iron deficiency is IV iron (sucrose, ferric carboxymaltose, ferric gluconate, ferumoxytol). This would be the correct answer in non-CKD iron deficiency without inflammation." }
    ],
    "correctAnswerIndex": 1,
    "clinicalPearl": "Key takeaway: ESA hyporesponsiveness in CKD — first check iron (ferritin <100 or transferrin sat <20% = need IV iron). Hepcidin from inflammation blocks oral iron absorption. Other causes: bleeding, hyperPTH, infection.",
